# uniq — real-world examples

`uniq` only collapses *adjacent* duplicates, so input almost always gets
sorted first. `sort -u` and `sort | uniq` have the same effect.

## Basic deduplication

```sh
# Show all lines without duplication
sort file | uniq

# Show only lines that appear exactly once
sort file | uniq -u

# Show only lines that appear more than once
sort file | uniq -d
```

## Counting

```sh
# Count occurrences of every line
sort file | uniq -c

# Count only non-duplicated lines
sort file | uniq -uc

# Count only duplicated lines
sort file | uniq -dc
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Top IPs hammering sshd on homelab (from journald)
journalctl -u sshd --since today | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \
  | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head

# Which shells are actually in use on this box?
cut -d: -f7 /etc/passwd | sort | uniq -c

# Find duplicate movie filenames across two media folders
ls /srv/media/movies /srv/media/movies-backup | sort | uniq -d

# Case-insensitive duplicate check on a wordlist
sort -f words.txt | uniq -di
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-c` | prefix lines with occurrence count |
| `-d` | print only duplicated lines (once each) |
| `-u` | print only unique (non-repeated) lines |
| `-i` | ignore case when comparing |
| `-f N` | skip the first N fields when comparing |
| `-s N` | skip the first N characters when comparing |
| `-w N` | compare at most N characters |
